AB  - The dramatic rise of artificial intelligence (AI) models has brought to the forefront the critical role of hardware-aware optimizations, particularly in leveraging the computational power of graphics processing units (GPUs). This chapter starts by tracing the evolution of GPUs from fixed-function graphics cards to generalized parallel processing units with specialized components like Tensor Cores and RT Cores, emphasizing the importance of FLOPS as a measure of compute power. It then delves into sophisticated memory management techniques, including in-place operations, memory sharing, and the strategic advantages of deep and thin network architectures. A significant portion of the chapter is dedicated to optimizing the attention mechanism through Flash Attention and addressing fault tolerance and memory constraints via Checkpointing and Gradient Checkpointing. Furthermore, the chapter explores various parallelism strategies essential for scaling large models, such as Data Parallelism with Ring All-Reduce and Gradient Compression, Tensor Parallelism, Pipeline Parallelism, and Context Parallelism. Finally, it covered a spectrum of inference speed optimizations, including model-level, architecture-level, inference-time, system-level, hardware-aware, and infrastructure-level accelerations like server-side batching, continuous batching with Paged Attention, prefill optimization, placement optimization, GPU virtualization, custom CUDA kernels, and Zero-Inference. This comprehensive discussion equips readers with the knowledge to efficiently train and deploy large AI models, overcoming the inherent challenges of memory, compute, and latency in modern hardware environments.
